// KJ: BO3-style flowmap water material template.
//
// This ties together two separate systems built this project:
//   - gameplay water physics (the "water" surfaceparm below gives this brush
//     CONTENTS_WATER, so idPhysics_Actor::SetWaterLevel picks it up for
//     swim/buoyancy/drowning - see the water physics work earlier)
//   - the visual flowmap shader (waterFlow.vs.hlsl/waterFlow.ps.hlsl)
//
// Duplicate this per distinct water surface in your map (different flow
// patterns, tint colors, tiling, etc. per instance) - it's a working
// EXAMPLE, not a single shared material every water brush should reference
// verbatim.
//
// Matches BO3's water shader texture set: colormap, normalmap, flowmap.
// Gloss isn't textured - BO3 water barely varies its gloss per-material, so
// it's a per-material constant (vertexParm 1.w) instead.
//
// Required source textures:
//   - a colormap (RGB = water color/tint - the same role BO3's colormap
//     plays: base water color, murkiness, foam/detail variation). Also
//     flows with the current, same as the normal map.
//   - a plain normal map (RGB = tangent-space normal, no packed alpha
//     channel needed - any of the standard normal map suffixes work, see
//     docs/PBR_MATERIALS.md).
//   - a flowmap (RG = flow direction, remapped from [0,1] storage to
//     [-1,1] in the shader). Paint one in an editor like Flow Map Painter,
//     or export from your DCC tool. A flat (128,128,0) gray texture reads
//     as "no flow" - a reasonable placeholder to start from.
//
// Tunable per-material parameters:
//   vertexParm 0   time, tint color rgb (multiplies the colormap - yzw)
//   vertexParm 1   screenDistortMagnitude, flowStrength, tiling, gloss
textures/liquid/mod_flowwater_example
{
	qer_editorimage textures/liquid/mod_flowwater_example_color.tga

	noshadows
	water
	twoSided
	translucent
	sort decal

	{
		vertexProgram   waterFlow.vs.hlsl
		fragmentProgram waterFlow.ps.hlsl

		// time (x), tint color rgb (yzw) - multiplies the colormap; leave at
		// white (1,1,1) to use the colormap's own colors unmodified, or push
		// toward green/yellow for slime-tinted water, blue for clear/deep
		// water, gray/brown for muddy water
		vertexParm 0   time, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0

		// screen distortion magnitude (x), flow strength (y), UV tiling (z),
		// gloss (w) - fixed per-material, 0 = fully rough/matte, 1 = mirror-
		// like; most water reads well somewhere around 0.7-0.85
		vertexParm 1   0.5, 0.06, 2.0, 0.8

		fragmentMap 0  _currentRender
		fragmentMap 1  textures/liquid/mod_flowwater_example_color.tga
		fragmentMap 2  textures/liquid/mod_flowwater_example_normal.tga
		fragmentMap 3  textures/liquid/mod_flowwater_example_flowmap.tga
	}
}
